>    I would be interested in knowing what feds mean by child pornography,
>and why they think it is bad. . . .
>[ Below are my guesses; I do not [necessarily] share any of these ideas,
>  just want to bring them up ]
> 
>  It seems that the CP may hurt :
>     1) the child who poses for pictures;
. . .
>  Now, posession and distribution of CP have nothing to do with 1). . . .
>- Alex Chislenko.

I think that CP regulators would disagree.  Their argument: We are
attacking the demand side of the CP market because we want to affect
supply.  Making it more costly to possess and distribute CP lowers demand. 
If there is less demand, there will be less supply.  And if there is less
supply, then there will be fewer instances of children being forced to
perform ponographic acts.

Please understand that I am not here advocating or denigrating this view; I
simply think that we ought to try to understand the justifications offered
for CP regulation.

Consider how this approach would apply to Andrew Hall's hypo:

>. . . does this mean
>that I could use some top line image processors and modify kiddie
>porn such that it was no longer a specific child, but an "ideal"
>combination of many children and this would legal?

Any product derived from CP would be regulated under the economic view I
have imputed to federal authorities.  It doesn't matter what the product
ends up looking like; what matters is that its raw inputs support the CP
trade.

An interesting question that I once posed to a speaker trying to justify CP
regulation under the economic theory:  what about CP cartoons?  If the evil
CP regulation aims to alleviate is the exploitation of children, how could
anyone object to CP products that do not involve kids as models or actors? 
This made the speaker squirm, for he was really arguing against CP because
he found it morally repugnant that some people enjoyed it--not just that
kids may be abused in its production.  The speaker *could have* argued that
CP cartoons stimulate demand for CP photos and films, but one could just as
easily argue that they serve to alleviate such demand by offering a cheaper
(because lower risk) alternative.


