Subject: POLI: Land reform

   From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)

   And the Church is often in collusion with the large landowners (who I doubt
   tithe very much each year!) and the State itself.

Personally, I suspect that the large landowners tithe as much or more
(as a percentage of income) as the pesants.  But like the income tax
here, a tax on income doesn't affect the distribution of wealth (asset
ownership) much.

   The Latin American experience tends to make me support forms of Marxist
   revolution (gasp!)....the Marxists take the "land grants" (huge parcels of
   land, millions of acres, given out by the Kings of Spain and Portugal and
   with the support of the Pope to toadying sycophants in the 17th and 18th
   centuries) away from the landowners and redistribute them (sometimes).
   Then, some years later, the Marxists themselves get thrown out and replaced
   by some form of a market economy. Sometimes land redistribution is needed.
   That's a fact.

   I don't claim to be a Marxist, natch. Nor do I support "land reform" in all
   of its forms (as in Cambodia, the collectivizations in Russia and China,
   etc.). I just note that sometime the fastest (best?) route from feudalistic
   ownership of State/Church-granted estates to a more "reasonable" (= smaller
   estates, free market in land ownership, etc.) system is through some form
   of Marxist revolution. There may be other routes, free market routes, of
   course. But I haven't seen many reported on.

I once saw an analysis that claimed that the Asian "Little Dragons"
had all been though a land reform -- Japan after WW II, Korea after
the Korean War, Taiwan after the Nationalists took over -- and that
the land reform was necessary for industrialization.  The author
(whoever it was) noted that one Asian country which has not
industrialized is the Philippines, which has not had land reform.
(China has also had land reform -- when Deng distributed the communes'
land to the pesants.)

A reasonably free-market route for land reform in El Salvador was
proposed by P. J. O'Rourke:  The U.S. should simply *buy* all of the
estates in El Salvador (worth about $100 million), and *give* the land
to the pesants.  Given the amount of money we've spent propping up the
current government, it would be a very cheap solution.

Dale

